    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b4e4d1dd",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Hateful; overall-intent-description: The image depicts two Black men in a rural setting, with one appearing to be in distress or discomfort while the other stands nearby. The text overlay reads 'please follow proper safety protocol' and 'osha is there for a reason,' which is a sarcastic or mocking reference to OSHA (Occupational Safety and Health Administration). The meme uses the visual of the men, who appear to be in a non-industrial, possibly impoverished environment, to imply that OSHA regulations are unnecessary or irrelevant in such contexts. This is a harmful and hateful stereotype that targets Black people by implying they are inherently unsafe or unqualified to follow safety protocols, and that their environment is inherently dangerous or 'unregulated.' The meme relies on racialized assumptions about Black communities being less capable of adhering to safety standards, which is a form of racial stereotyping and discrimination. The juxtaposition of the image with the text creates a demeaning message that devalues the lives and safety concerns of Black individuals, suggesting that their circumstances are so dire that they don't deserve the same safety protections as others. This is a clear example of a hateful meme that uses racial stereotypes to justify or mock the perceived lack of safety in Black communities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_0686f111"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
